"Linearization Explains Fine-Tuning in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08239v1)\nciteturn31656view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view2\",\"lineno\":279}); Total lines: 810\nL275: We now demonstrate that insights from our theory apply in practice, despite moving from squared loss to cross-entropy and from gradient descent to the AdamW optimizer, which we use for all fine-tuning in conjunction with the selective regularization (cite57†8 ). In our experiments, we implement LoRA on RoBERTa base and evaluate its performance on the General Language Understanding Evaluation (GLUE) benchmark [cite70†20 ], IMDb [cite71†21 ], and Yelp [cite72†22 ] datasets.\nL276: ### 6.1 Model, Datasets and Optimizer\nL277: \nL278: RoBERTa base incorporates several key modifications to the pretraining process, such as using larger batch sizes, longer sequences, and more diverse data than its antecedents like BERT [cite73†23 ]. Despite its relatively compact size of $125$M parameters, RoBERTa base has proven to be one of the most powerful models for various NLP tasks, including text classification, question answering, and named entity recognition, especially on the GLUE benchmark.\nL279: The GLUE benchmark is a collection of diverse tasks that test a model’s natural language understanding abilities. The tasks included in our experiments are linguistic acceptability judgment (CoLA [cite74†24 ]) and sentiment analysis (SST-2 [cite75†25 ]). The GLUE benchmark provides a comprehensive evaluation of a model’s performance across various NLP challenges, assessing its ability to understand and reason about language in different contexts.\nL280: The IMDb dataset is a large dataset for binary sentiment classification, containing $50$k highly popular movie reviews from the Internet Movie Database (IMDb). The Yelp dataset contains customer reviews from Yelp, a popular platform for crowd-sourced reviews about businesses, primarily restaurants. This dataset originally contains reviews with ratings from 1 to 5.\nL281: To convert it into a binary classification task, we consider reviews with ratings less than 3 as label 0 (negative sentiment) and those with ratings greater than or equal to 3 as label 1 (positive sentiment).\nL282: Table cite76†9 in Appendix cite30†N shows specific hyperparameters for RoBERTa base across various benchmarks, including GLUE tasks (CoLA, SST-2), Yelp, and IMDb. For all experiments, we use LoRA on the RoBERTa-base model from the Hugging Face transformers library [cite77†26 ], and report its performance on different tasks. We implemented our code on NVIDIA Tesla V100 GPUs. Following Hu et al.\nL283: ($2022$), we mostly use the weights of the query and value layers, $\\mathbf{W}_{q}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times p}$ and $\\mathbf{W}_{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times p}$ for fine-tuning. In our experiments, we apply LoRA with $r=8$, which has $(m+p)\\times r=2\\times 768\\times 8$ trainable parameters per selected layer for each of the query, key, and value projection matrices in the self-attention mechanism in the RoBERTa base model.\nL284: cite78†Image: Refer to caption (a) Train loss over 10 epochs\nL285: \nL286: cite79†Image: Refer to caption (b) Condition number\nL287: \nL288: cite80†Image: Refer to caption (c) Evaluation accuracy and Condition number\nL289: Figure 2: (a)-(b) Illustrate the positive correlation between the convergence rate of optimization steps of LoRA over $10$ epochs and $\\kappa(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})$ of NTK at initialization. $\\{\\mathbf{W}_{q},\\mathbf{W}_{v}\\}$ of layers $\\{0,5,11\\}$ are fine-tuned. (c) Illustrates the negative correlation between evaluation accuracy after 10 epochs of training and the condition number of NTK. LoRA with $r=8$ is used to fine-tune $\\{\\mathbf{W}_{k}\\}$ of the layers $\\{0,5,11\\}$.\nL290: ### 6.2 Linearization of Fine-Tuning\nL291: In Table cite61†1 , we analyze how regularization impacts the fine-tuning of models on the CoLA and SST-2 datasets, using several metrics, including the deviation of fine-tuned parameters from the pretrained parameters, $\\|\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\|$, KL divergence between predictions of the regularized fine-tuned model and its linearized counterpart, the $\\ell_{2}$ norm $\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\bar{f}_{\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}}(\\mathbf{X})\\|_{2}$, evaluation accuracy.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Statistical Framework for Alignment with Biased AI Feedback (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08259v1)\nciteturn31656view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view3\",\"lineno\":278}); Total lines: 458\nL272: where, with probability at least $1-\\delta$,\nL273: \nL274:  | $$|R|\\leq C\\!\\left(\\frac{\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|}{\\sqrt{N}}+\\frac{\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|+\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}-1\\|}{\\epsilon\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\\!\\sqrt{\\log(1/\\delta)}+\\frac{\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|\\cdot\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}-1\\|}{\\epsilon^{2}}.$$  |\nL275: The expansion in Theorem cite70†2 decomposes the DIPO estimator into three leading components and a remainder term. The first term corresponds to a direct plug-in estimator of the preference probability. The second term captures the variance contribution arising from bias correction using human-labeled data. When $N\\gg n$, this component typically dominates the stochastic fluctuation.\nL276: The third term reflects additional variance induced by using a single realization of the AI evaluator; this contribution can be reduced by averaging multiple evaluations or by employing deterministic evaluation scores.\nL277: The remainder term $R$ depends on the sample sizes $(n,N)$ and on the quality of the nuisance estimators $(\\widehat{g},\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}})$. The terms involving $\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|$ arise from imperfect AI evaluation, while the term involving $\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}-1\\|$ reflects error in estimating the generation policy. When the generation mechanism is known, this term vanishes.\nL278: The final product term captures the interaction between these two sources of error and is characteristic of debiased estimation procedures (cite61†Chernozhukov et al., 2018 ).\nL279: We next compare the efficiency of $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi)$ with that of an IPO estimator based solely on human-labeled data, and show that $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi)$ achieves optimal estimation efficiency.\nL280: ###### Corollary 1 (Efficiency gain over human-only IPO).\nL281: Under the conditions of Theorem cite70†2 , suppose $\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|=o(1)$, $\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}-1\\|=o(1)$, and $\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|\\cdot\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}-1\\|=o(n^{-1/2})$. Assume $N>n$ and that the AI evaluator provides exact preference probabilities or sufficiently accurate Monte Carlo approximations.\nL282: Let $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{IPO}}(\\pi)$ denote the IPO estimator based only on $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{Human}}$. Then\nL283:  | $$\\mathrm{MSE}(\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi))<\\mathrm{MSE}(\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{IPO}}(\\pi)),\\qquad n,N\\to\\infty.$$  |\nL284: \nL285: Moreover, when $N\\gg n$, the estimator $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi)$ attains the semiparametric efficiency bound. In particular, it achieves the minimum asymptotic variance and mean squared error among all regular estimators of $P(\\pi(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot))$.\nL286: \nL287: Finally, we establish a regret bound for the resulting policy estimator.\nL288: ###### Theorem 3 (Regret bound for DIPO).\nL289: \nL290: Under the conditions of Theorem cite70†2 and Assumption cite57†3 , suppose $\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|\\to 0$ and $\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}-1\\|\\to 0$. Then\nL291:  |  | $\\displaystyle P(\\pi^{*}(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot))-P(\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot))$  |\nL292:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\quad=O\\!\\left(\\beta+\\sqrt{v\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{1}{N}\\right)}+v\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{1}{N}\\right)+\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}-1\\|\\cdot\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|\\right).$  |\nL293: Theorem cite71†3 derives the regret bound of DIPO and highlights several key factors influencing the performance of the proposed DIPO estimator. First, the bound decreases as the sample size increases, reflecting the expected improvement in performance as more preference data become available. Second, it grows with the regularization parameter $\\beta$ in the KL penalty, indicating that $\\beta$ should be chosen sufficiently small to limit its impact.\nL294: Most importantly, the dependence on estimation errors in the reference policy and the preference model appears only through the product $\\|\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}-1\\|\\,\\|\\tilde{g}-g\\|$ which is different from the regret of DDPO. This structure substantially reduces the impact of misspecification in either component and plays a central role in the robustness of the proposed method.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNear-Oracle KV Selection via Pre-hoc Sparsity for Long-Context Inference (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08329v1)\nciteturn31656view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31650view2\",\"lineno\":180}); Total lines: 994\nL161:  | $$A_{i}^{h}(\\mathbf{q}_{t}^{h})\\!=\\!\\frac{\\exp\\!\\big((\\mathbf{q}_{t}^{h})^{\\top}\\mathbf{k}_{i}^{h}/\\sqrt{d}\\big)}{\\sum_{j=1}^{t}\\exp\\!\\big((\\mathbf{q}_{t}^{h})^{\\top}\\mathbf{k}_{j}^{h}/\\sqrt{d}\\big)},\\;\\mathbf{y}_{t}^{h}\\!=\\!\\sum_{i=1}^{t}A_{i}^{h}(\\mathbf{q}_{t}^{h})\\,\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{h}.$$  |  | (2)\nL162: \nL163: Since all heads operate the same, we omit the head index for brevity in the following context, unless explicitly mentioned.\nL164: ##### Prefill\nL165: \nL166: The prompt is processed once to construct and cache $(\\mathbf{K},\\mathbf{V})$, which is reused during subsequent decoding [cite130†23 ].\nL167: \nL168: ##### Decode\nL169: \nL170: Only the new token is processed while reusing the cached $\\mathbf{K}$ and $\\mathbf{V}$ from previous steps for each head.\nL171: ##### Bottlenecks\nL172: \nL173: From Eq. (cite131†2 ), the per-step attention FLOPs (score computation and value aggregation) scale as $\\mathcal{O}(Htd)$, and the KV cache also scales as $\\mathcal{O}(Htd)$. This linear growth in $t$ constitutes the primary compute and memory bottleneck for long-context inference.\nL174: TABLE I: Summary of key variables and notation.\nL175: $\\mathbf{x}$  | Input token embedding  | $\\mathbf{q},\\,\\mathbf{k},\\,\\mathbf{v}$  | Query, key, value\nL176: $L$  | Input length  | $d$  | Per-head dimension\nL177: $H$  | Number of heads  | $A(\\cdot)$  | Attention weight\nL178: $\\mathbf{K},\\mathbf{V}$  | KV cache matrices  | $t$  | Step count\nL179: $\\mathcal{S}$  | Selector returning a subset of indices  | $S_{t}$  | Selected KV index set at step $t$\nL180: $N$  | KV budget (sparsity: $N/t$)  | $\\tau(\\cdot)$  | Retained attention mass\nL181: $\\delta(\\cdot)$  | Dropped attention mass  | $I_{\\mathrm{full}},I_{\\mathcal{S}}$  | MI under full attention vs. TSA\nL182: $g(\\cdot)$  | MI-loss bound function  | $\\delta^{*}(\\cdot)$  | Oracle (top-$k$) dropped mass\nL183: ### II-B Token-sparse Attention and Key-value Sharing\nL184: KV compression mitigates the aforementioned bottlenecks by exploiting attention sparsity—a small subset of critical KV entries is selected to approximate full attention. This improves efficiency at the cost of approximation error.\nL185: To evaluate accuracy–efficiency trade-offs across KV compression methods, and following prior work [cite132†44 , cite119†43 , cite126†42 , cite133†17 ], we formalize decoding with a limited KV set under a unified token-sparse attention (TSA) concept, and then define KV sharing as a special case that streamlines TSA selection.\nL186: Definition 3.1 (Token-sparse attention, informal). At decoding step $t$, with KV budget $N<t$, a selector $\\mathcal{S}$ outputs an index set $S_{t}\\subseteq[t]\\coloneqq\\{1,\\dots,t\\}$ with $|S_{t}|=N$. The corresponding key/value matrices are restricted to these indices, $\\mathbf{K}_{S_{t}}$ and $\\mathbf{V}_{S_{t}}$. Attention is computed only between $\\mathbf{q}_{t}$ and $\\mathbf{K}_{S_{t}}$ (cf. Eq. (cite131†2 )), and the output uses $\\mathbf{V}_{S_{t}}$. The sparsity ratio at step $t$ is $N/t$.\nL187: Definition 3.2 (Key-value sharing). Given indices $S_{i}$ retrieved by $\\mathcal{S}$ at an anchor step $i$ in TSA, a later token at step $j$ may directly reuse them, denoted $S_{j}\\leftarrow S_{i}$. In this case, $S_{j}$ is utilized to construct $\\mathbf{K}_{S_{j}}$ and $\\mathbf{V}_{S_{j}}$.\nL188: Discussion. Different TSA instantiations correspond to different selectors $\\mathcal{S}$, and exhibit distinct accuracy–efficiency trade-offs under a fixed budget. For example, imprecise selection that admits many low-contribution pairs can crowd out salient entries, increasing approximation error and degrading model performance. This failure mode is analogous to erroneous KV sharing across steps whose truly critical sets have little overlap.\nL189: Moreover, selectors rely on different criteria [cite120†19 , cite113†49 , cite118†28 ] (e.g., similarity heuristics, learned scores, recency rules), and the mechanism used to enforce these criteria determines selection complexity and entry-retrieval efficiency. Consequently, an effective TSA selector must balance accuracy (retained mass) and retrieval overhead (selection complexity).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOJBKQ: Objective-Joint Babai-Klein Quantization (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08376v1)\nciteturn31656view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31650view3\",\"lineno\":97}); Total lines: 516\nL92: A recurring challenge in layer-wise PTQ is that minimizing a layer-local reconstruction proxy does not necessarily translate to improved end-to-end quality, due to quantization error propagation and the resulting input distribution drift across layers. Consequently, the choice of the alignment target—whether to match outputs under runtime (partially-quantized) activations or to match full-precision reference outputs—plays a central role in practical PTQ performance.\nL93: Recent work revisits this issue from an error-propagation viewpoint and advocates objectives that better reflect downstream behavior under partial quantization (cite44†Arai and Ichikawa, 2025 ). Our JTA objective follows this perspective but provides a unified scoring criterion with a single continuous knob that interpolates between runtime-quantized activations and full-precision reference targets, enabling consistent candidate selection across layers.\nL94: ## 3 Methodology\nL95: ### 3.1 Layer-wise Objectives and Joint Target Alignment (JTA)\nL96: We now start analyze commonly used layer-wise PTQ objectives. Consider a linear layer with full-precision weight matrix $\\mathbf{W}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}$.\nL97: Let $\\mathbf{X}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p\\times m}$ be full-precision calibration activations, $\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p\\times m}$ be the corresponding runtime activations produced by a partially quantized network (i.e., upstream layers already quantized), $\\mathbf{Y}^{\\mathrm{fp}}:=\\mathbf{XW}$ denote the full-precision reference output, and $\\mathbf{Y}^{\\mathrm{rt}}:=\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\mathbf{W}$ denote the runtime-consistent reference output under partially quantized activations.\nL98: At runtime, a candidate $\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}$ produces the output $\\widehat{\\mathbf{Y}}:=\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}$.\nL99: Many existing objectives can be interpreted as choosing different alignment targets:\nL100:  | (Runtime-consistent)     | $\\displaystyle\\min_{\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}}\\;\\;\\|\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}-\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\mathbf{W}\\|_{F}^{2}$  |  | (1)\nL101:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\qquad=\\|\\widehat{\\mathbf{Y}}-\\mathbf{Y}^{\\mathrm{rt}}\\|_{F}^{2},$  |  | (2)\nL102:  | (Full-precision mapping)  | $\\displaystyle\\min_{\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}}\\;\\;\\|\\mathbf{X}\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}-\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{W}\\|_{F}^{2},$  |  | (3)\nL103:  | (Mismatch target)  | $\\displaystyle\\min_{\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}}\\;\\;\\|\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}-\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{W}\\|_{F}^{2}$  |  | (4)\nL104:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\qquad=\\|\\widehat{\\mathbf{Y}}-\\mathbf{Y}^{\\mathrm{fp}}\\|_{F}^{2}.$  |  | (5)\nL105: Eq. (cite61†1 ) directly reflects runtime behavior but aligns to a target computed on quantized activations, which may be noisy; this objective is adopted by GPTQ and QUIP. Eq. (cite62†3 ) admits a convenient least-squares structure (prior to discretization) but suffers from train–run mismatch when earlier layers are quantized, and is optimized by AWQ.\nL106: Eq. (cite63†4 ) partially alleviates this mismatch by evaluating candidates on runtime activations while preserving the full-precision reference; QEP employs this objective as a corrective patch within a layer-wise quantization pipeline, although it may over-penalize candidates when error drift accumulates across layers.\nL107: These trade-offs motivate a unified selection objective that interpolates between runtime-consistent and full-precision references, while optionally controlling weight drift.\nL108: \nL109: We propose an expressive Joint Target Alignment (JTA) objective with a single continuous knob to score and select candidates generated by Random-$K$ decoding. We define an interpolated target\nL110:  | $$\\small\\mathbf{Y}^{\\star}(\\mu):=(1-\\mu)\\,\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{W}+\\mu\\,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\mathbf{W},\\hskip 18.49988pt\\mu\\in[0,1],$$  |  | (6)\nL111: \nL112: and formulate the ILS problem:\nL113: \nL114:  | $$\\min\\mathcal{S}(\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}})\\;:=\\;\\|\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}-\\mathbf{Y}^{\\star}(\\mu)\\|_{F}^{2}\\;+\\;\\lambda^{2}\\|\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}-\\mathbf{W}\\|_{F}^{2},$$  |  | (7)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLinearization Explains Fine-Tuning in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08239v1)\nciteturn31656view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view2\",\"pattern\":\"H Proof\"}); Total lines: 810\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Problem Formulation L18:   4. cite8†3 Proximity to the Pretrained Model Promotes Linearity L19:   5. cite9†4 Fine-Tuning Meets Neural Tangent Kernel Regression L20:   6. cite10†5 Spectral Perturbation of Layers L21:   7. cite11†6 Experiments L22:     1. cite12†6.1 Model, Datasets and Optimizer L23:     2. cite13†6.2 Linearization of Fine-Tuning L24:     3. cite14†6.3 NTK Evaluation L25:   8. cite15†7 Conclusion and Limitations L26:   9. cite16†References L27:   10. cite17†A Definitions and Lemmas L28:   11. cite18†B Proof of Theorem   L29:   12. cite19†C Proof of Theorem L30:   13. cite20†D Proof of Theorem L31:   14. cite21†E Proof of Theorem L32:   15. cite22†F Proof of Theorem L33:   16. cite23†G Proof of Theorem L34:   17. cite24†H Proof of Theorem L35:   18. cite25†I Robustness to Sampling for Estimating the NTK L36:   19. cite26†J Additional Experiments L37:   20. cite27†K Lipschitzness L38:   21. cite28†L Layer Selection Algorithm L39:   22. cite29†M Discussion and Implication of Assumptions L40:   23. cite30†N Hyperparameters L41: cite31†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L42: \nL43: arXiv:2602.08239v1 [cs.LG] 09 Feb 2026\nL44: # Linearization Explains Fine-Tuning in Large Language Models\nL45: Zahra Rahimi Afzal ^{†}^{†}thanks: Equal contribution. Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago Affiliation: {zrahim2, msol, mesrob}@uic.edu    Tara Esmaeilbeig^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1 Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago Affiliation: {zrahim2, msol, mesrob}@uic.edu    Mojtaba Soltanalian Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago Affiliation: {zrahim2, msol, mesrob}@uic.edu    Mesrob I.\nL46: Ohannessian Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago Affiliation: {zrahim2, msol, mesrob}@uic.edu Affiliation: Nokia Bell Labs Affiliation: tara.esmaeilbeig@nokia.com\nL47: ###### Abstract\nL48: Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) is a popular class of techniques that strive to adapt large models in a scalable and resource-efficient manner. Yet, the mechanisms underlying their training performance and generalization remain underexplored. In this paper, we provide several insights into such fine-tuning through the lens of linearization. Fine-tuned models are often implicitly encouraged to remain close to the pretrained model.\nL49: By making this explicit, using an $\\ell_{2}$-distance inductive bias in parameter space, we show that fine-tuning dynamics become equivalent to learning with the positive-definite neural tangent kernel (NTK). We specifically analyze how close the fully linear and the linearized fine-tuning optimizations are, based on the strength of the regularization. This allows us to be pragmatic about how good a model linearization is when fine-tuning large language models (LLMs).\nL50: When linearization is a good model, our findings reveal a strong correlation between the eigenvalue spectrum of the NTK and the performance of model adaptation. Motivated by this, we give spectral perturbation bounds on the NTK induced by the choice of layers selected for fine-tuning. We empirically validate our theory on Low Rank Adaptation (LoRA) on LLMs.\nL51: These insights not only characterize fine-tuning but also have the potential to enhance PEFT techniques, paving the way to better informed and more nimble adaptation in LLMs. ^{2}^{2}footnotetext: Our code is publicly available at cite32†https://github.com/zahrahimi/linearization†github.com .\nL592: ## Appendix G Proof of Theorem cite68†6 L593: \nL594: Since $\\mathbf{K}$ is positive semidefinite (see Lemma cite135†2 ), we have\nL595: \nL596:  | $$\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}=\\mathbf{K}^{1/2}\\left(\\mathbf{I}+\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\mathbf{S}\\;\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\right)\\mathbf{K}^{1/2}.$$  |  | (88)\nL597: \nL598: Considering $\\eta=\\|\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\mathbf{S}\\;\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\|$, we have\nL599: \nL600:  | $$-\\eta\\mathbf{I}\\leq\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\mathbf{S}\\;\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\leq\\eta\\mathbf{I}.$$  |  | (89)\nL601: \nL602: Consequently,\nL603:  | $$(1-\\eta)\\mathbf{I}\\leq\\mathbf{I}+\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\mathbf{S}\\;\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\leq(1+\\eta)\\mathbf{I},$$  |  | (90)\nL604: \nL605: and, from (cite136†88 ) and (cite137†90 ), we obtain\nL606: \nL607:  | $$(1-\\eta)\\mathbf{K}\\leq\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}\\leq(1+\\eta)\\mathbf{K}.$$  |  | (91)\nL608: \nL609: Note that for any two positive semidefinite matrices $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$, if $\\mathbf{A}\\leq\\mathbf{B}$, then $\\lambda_{i}(\\mathbf{A})\\leq\\lambda_{i}(\\mathbf{B})$. Therefore, (cite138†91 ) is equivalent to (cite139†20 ) [cite140†29 ].\nL610: ## Appendix H Proof of Theorem cite86†7 L611: \nL612: From Theorem  cite67†5 , we already know\nL613: \nL614:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\left(\\lambda_{\\text{min}}(\\mathbf{K})+\\sigma\\right)^{2}}{\\sigma^{2}\\|\\mathbf{Y}\\|^{2}}\\leq\\frac{1}{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta})}\\leq\\frac{\\left(\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K})+\\sigma\\right)^{2}}{\\sigma^{2}\\|\\mathbf{Y}\\|^{2}}.$  |  | (92)\nL615: \nL616: Theorem cite68†6 , states that\nL617:  | $$\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K})+\\sigma=\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})\\leq\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{1-\\eta}$$  |  | (93)\nL618: \nL619: and\nL620: \nL621:  | $$\\lambda_{\\text{min}}(\\mathbf{K})+\\sigma=\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{\\kappa(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}\\geq\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{\\kappa(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})(1+\\eta)}.$$  |  | (94)\nL622: \nL623: Therefore, we have\nL624:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{\\sigma\\|\\mathbf{Y}\\|\\kappa(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})(1+\\eta)}\\leq\\frac{1}{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta})^{\\frac{1}{2}}}\\leq\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{\\sigma\\|\\mathbf{Y}\\|(1-\\eta)}.$  |  | (95)\nL625: \nL626: On the other hand, it follows from Theorem cite67†5 that\nL627:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\sigma\\|\\mathbf{Y}\\|}{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S})+\\sigma}\\leq\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta}\\cup\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}})^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\leq\\frac{\\sigma\\|\\mathbf{Y}\\|}{\\lambda_{\\text{min}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S})+\\sigma},$  |  | (96)\nL628: \nL629: and, from Theorem cite68†6 that\nL630:  | $\\displaystyle\\lambda_{\\text{min}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S})+\\sigma$  | $\\displaystyle\\geq(1-\\eta)\\lambda_{\\text{min}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})$  |\nL631:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{(1-\\eta)\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{\\kappa({\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I}})},$  |\nL632:  | $\\displaystyle\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S})+\\sigma$  | $\\displaystyle\\leq(1+\\eta)\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I}).$  |  | (97)\nL633: \nL634: From (cite141†96 ) and (cite142†97 ), we conclude\nL635:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\sigma\\|\\mathbf{Y}\\|}{(1+\\eta)\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}\\leq\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta}\\cup\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}})^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\leq\\frac{\\sigma\\|\\mathbf{Y}\\|\\kappa(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{(1-\\eta)\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}.$  |  | (98)\nL636: \nL637: Considering that $\\kappa(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})\\leq c$, the inequalities (cite143†98 ) and (cite144†95 ) imply\nL638:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{c(1+\\eta)^{2}\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}\\leq\\left(\\frac{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta}\\cup\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}})}{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta})}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\leq\\frac{c\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{(1-\\eta)^{2}\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}.$  |  | (99)\n"
